  "description": [
    {
      "@value": "Hunger is an adaptive motivational state that drives individuals toward homeostatic balance via food intake (Saper et al., 2002). However, it can also trigger anger and social aggression, a phenomenon colloquially known as \u201cbeing hangry\" (Swami, 2022). Research supports that hunger influences emotion and decision-making (Hausser et al., 2019; Swami et al., 2022; Swami et al., 2022). In non-human species, food deprivation increases aggression to secure food, while in humans, it is linked to restlessness, irritability, and behavioral difficulties (Manello et al., 1967; Slack et al., 2005). Traditional explanations attribute \"hangriness\" to ego depletion, a lack of energy that impairs self-regulation and increases reactivity (Baumeister et al., 1998; DeWall et al., 2011) However, the ego depletion framework is increasingly criticized due to conceptual issues and failures to replicate (Dang, 2019).\nTo promote survival, food intake and hunger require causal inference, making a connection between causes and consequences. Traditionally, ingestive behavior, aimed at reducing hunger, has been conceptualized as immediately rewarding. However, reward from ingestion must be inferred from contextual and internal signals (Weber et al., 2024). Although eating (cause) reduces hunger (consequence), this relationship includes uncertainties. m time delays (minutes to hours) between ingestion and satiation. Structural uncertainties arises when multiple causes are available to explain one consequence. For instance, both high- and low-caloric foods reduce hunger, but only the former provide sustained energy. Biologically, learning to associate specific cues with hunger reduction and energy involves complex multisensory processing and dopaminergic reward signaling (Grove et al., 2022; Tellez et al., 2013; Tellez et al., 2013; Thanarajah et al., 2019). Through trial-and-error learning, specific cues become associated with hunger reduction and energization and thus acquire positive value, indicated by dopaminergic reward activity. Even hunger itself is part of a causal inference problem. \u201cHunger\u201d neurons do not directly drive \u201creward\u201d neurons, but rather modulate dopaminergic activity by signaling an aversive \u201cneed\u201d state (Reed et al., 2022). Thus, these hunger neurons are centrally involved in the anticipation of future states and behaviors.\nVia involvement of the dopaminergic reward system, hunger reflects a state of increased sensitivity to external cues, offering perfect conditions for enhanced causal inference, but not necessarily lowered impulse control (De Ridder et al., 2014; Swieten et al., 2021; Wang &amp; Dvorak, 2010).\nIn an EEG study, which is based on our preregistered behavioral study and earlier work of our group (Wurm &amp; G\u00fcr\u00fcn, 2026, Wurm et al. 2024), we will investigate the neural correlates of feedback processing and how they are modulated by hunger. Interestingly, recent research supports the idea that hungry participants show increased experience-driven but not goal-driven learning, increased delay discounting, and advantageous gambling choices (De Ridder et al., 2014; Swieten et al., 2021; Wang and Dvorak, 2010). We expect to find hallmarks for this altered experience-driven learning in central ERP components associated with dopaminergic activity and outcome expectancy. \nA direct implication from the expected results is that hunger changes the way humans sample and utilize evidence for everyday decision-making. It would suggest a reduction in necessary evidence and thus increased risk of \u201cjumping to conclusions\u201d. This will open new avenues for research into the cognitive and behavioral consequences of hunger. Additionally, this project is an important starting point for understanding (psycho)pathologies linked to food intake and hunger."
    }
  ],
